## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to justify product bets with a pragmatic, numbers-driven approach and to handle uncertainty in inputs.

## Key Concepts

- Cost estimation
- Benefit estimation
- Break-even analysis
- Reasonable assumptions

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CBA calculator
function cba(params) {
  const {maU, lift, arpu, months, cost} = params;
  const annualizedBenefit = maU * lift * arpu * months;
  return {annualizedBenefit, cost, breakevenMonths: cost / (annualizedBenefit || 1)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust if MAU grows or adoption changes?
- What if ARPU uplift is not uniform across user segments?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, new angle on CBA: on-device prefetching to cut latency, balancing storage, energy, privacy, and business impact. Tests the ability to translate UX improvements into measurable costs and decision criteria.

## Key Concepts

- On-device predictor energy/compute
- Cache budgeting and invalidation strategies
- Data transfer vs. storage costs
- Privacy/opt-in and data minimization
- Latency impact and engagement metrics
- Break-even analysis and go/no-go thresholds

## Code Example

```javascript
function calcBreakeven(params){
  const {users, cacheMB, energyPerHit, savedMsPerHit, valuePerMs, pricePerMB} = params;
  const energyCost = energyPerHit * users;
  const dataValue = savedMsPerHit * users * valuePerMs;
  const breakevenMonths = (cacheMB * pricePerMB) / ((dataValue) - energyCost);
  return breakevenMonths;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you calibrate the model without A/B tests for small pilots?
- What privacy constraints would you enforce for on-device prefetching data?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to reason about localized data processing, privacy controls, and cost trade-offs for a beginner-level feature.

## Key Concepts

- On-device data processing and storage budgeting
- Delta synchronization and telemetry cost control
- Privacy by design and opt-in controls
- Staged rollout with measurable go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
function deltaSize(prev, curr) {
  const delta = {};
  for (const k of Object.keys(curr)) {
    delta[k] = Math.max(0, (curr[k] || 0) - (prev[k] || 0));
  }
  return delta;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle data conflicts when multiple devices sync simultaneously?
- What fallback mechanisms would you implement if delta sync fails?
- How would you optimize the storage algorithm to handle bursty data patterns?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design cost-aware, privacy-preserving on-device learning with federation. Evaluate cross-app data leverage, regulatory implications, and rollout strategy across regions.

## Key Concepts

- Federated learning and secure aggregation
- On-device resource budgeting (CPU, memory, energy)
- Model drift telemetry and anomaly tracking
- Regulatory/compliance costs and rollout governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: federated update cycle outline
while (serviceRunning) {
  const localBatch = collectLocalSignals();
  const grads = computeGradients(localBatch);
  const encGrads = secureAggregate(grads);
  sendToServer(encGrads);
  updateLocalModelIfNeeded();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift and trigger model refresh?
- What telemetry would you collect to validate ROI without exposing data?

## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design privacy-preserving retention controls at the device edge, balancing storage, CPU, and regulatory costs while quantifying the business impact of data minimization.

## Key Concepts

- On-device policy engines and interpreters
- Data minimization and retention windows
- Batch policy delivery and anonymized audit deltas
- Compliance costs and telemetry design

## Code Example

```javascript
// Policy evaluator
function shouldDelete(event, policy) {
  const rule = policy.findRule(event.type);
  return event.timestamp < Date.now() - policy.getRetentionPeriod(rule);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle on CBA: gating cross‑app features via an on‑device policy engine, balancing UX, energy, and safety with cloud updates.

## Key Concepts
- On‑device policy engines and gating rules
- Resource awareness (battery, thermal, memory)
- Telemetry footprint and privacy compliance
- Rollout strategy and break‑even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function shouldRunFeature(state, policy) {
  if (state.battery < 20) return false;
  if (state.temperature > 45) return false;
  if (state.freeMemory < 200) return false;
  return policy.allowsFeature;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify risk of safety incidents vs false positives?
- What metrics would drive policy update cadence and rollouts across device generations?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to translate product goals into a concrete cost model that accounts for on-device constraints, security requirements, and regulatory concerns. It also probes understanding of trade-offs for multi-app isolation, update mechanics, and privacy-by-design principles.

## Key Concepts
- On-device ML model footprints and TEE (Trusted Execution Environment) architecture
- Cross-app isolation with per-app policy overrides
- Telemetry budgeting and data minimization under privacy regulations
- OTA update economics, delta patching, and cryptographic key rotation
- Compliance frameworks (GDPR, CCPA), privacy-by-design, and break-even analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-break-even calculator
function needsGoNoGo(serverSavingsPerMonth, deviceCostsPerMonth, months) {
  const netSavings = serverSavingsPerMonth - deviceCostsPerMonth;
  const cumulativeSavings = netSavings * months;
  return {
    breakEvenMonths: deviceCostsPerMonth / netSavings,
    go: cumulativeSavings > 0 && months >= 18
  };
}
```
